VisWiz WooCommerce Visualizer
=============================

Version 2.0.0 — Dataset-first architecture
------------------------------------------

VisWiz 2 is a structural rewrite of the 1.x plugin. The principal design rule is simple: **datasets own data; visualizations render data**.

### What changed

- `viswiz.php` is now a small bootstrap with a namespaced `VisWiz\\` application under `src/`.
- Plugin version and database schema version are independent (`VISWIZ_VERSION` / `VISWIZ_DB_VERSION`).
- Canonical dataset schemas are separate from renderers:
  - schemas: categorical, time series, X/Y, geographic, progress, graph, diagram
  - renderers: pie, bar, column, line, area, scatter, progress, counter, timeline, map, graph, flow diagram, org chart, diagram
- A categorical dataset can therefore be reused by Pie, Bar or Column visualizations without duplicating the data.
- Visualizations no longer keep a second authoritative copy of dataset graph data in post meta.

### New persistence model

VisWiz 2 introduces dedicated tables for:

- datasets
- generic rows
- graph nodes
- graph relations/edges
- dataset revisions

Graph relations reference **immutable node UUIDs**, not editable slugs. Renaming a node or changing its readable slug no longer requires relation endpoint remapping.

Every dataset carries a monotonically increasing revision. Targeted node/row/relation changes use optimistic concurrency; a stale editor receives a conflict instead of overwriting newer work.

Full delete-and-reinsert is restricted to deliberate dataset replace/import/restore operations and is wrapped in a database transaction. Normal node, row and relation editing performs targeted writes.

### Dataset revisions

Every mutation stores a dataset snapshot with:

- revision number
- actor user ID
- note
- timestamp

The Dataset editor exposes revision history and restore actions. Restore itself creates a new revision, so the state being replaced remains recoverable.

### Graph schema security

Global Node Types and Relation Types are protected by the dedicated `manage_viswiz_schema` capability. Dataset editors can assign existing types but cannot approve or mutate the global schema through a node autosave path.

The default roles are:

- Administrator: visualization editing, dataset editing, global schema/settings/update management
- Editor: visualization editing and dataset editing, but not global schema/settings/update management

### WooCommerce / HPOS

The old anonymous generic endpoints (`/sales`, `/sales-breakdown`, `/sales-status`) are removed.

Published live WooCommerce visualizations are now served only through:

`GET /wp-json/viswiz/v2/visualizations/{id}`

The request contains only the saved visualization ID. Visitors cannot supply arbitrary periods, product IDs or category IDs.

Live WooCommerce queries and snapshot generation share the same `SalesQuery` service:

- WooCommerce CRUD/query APIs only
- paginated `wc_get_orders()` calls (200 IDs/page)
- paid order statuses
- bounded date windows
- server-side transient cache
- product and category filtering at line-item level
- variation-aware product IDs
- total/month/product/status grouping
- revenue/order-count/item-quantity metrics
- created/paid/completed date basis
- explicit gross / item-net / item-gross revenue basis
- refund deduction for order gross totals

VisWiz declares HPOS compatibility through WooCommerce `FeaturesUtil`.

### Front-end rendering

The public runtime no longer depends on D3 or a third-party CDN. Renderers are implemented locally in `assets/viswiz.js`.

Implemented renderer paths are distinct rather than routing all chart-like types through Pie:

- Pie
- Bar
- Column
- Line
- Area
- Scatter
- Progress
- Counter
- Timeline
- Map (coordinate/equirectangular marker view)
- Graph
- Flow Diagram
- Org Chart
- Diagram

Graph, Flow and Org Chart share graph primitives but use different layout strategies.

Live data polling:

- is never faster than one minute
- uses server-side cached results
- starts only when the visualization approaches the viewport
- pauses while the document is hidden
- deduplicates simultaneous requests to the same endpoint in the browser

VisWiz CSS/JS is enqueued only when a VisWiz visualization is actually rendered.

### Gutenberg

The `viswiz/visualization` dynamic block is registered from `blocks/visualization/block.json` (Block API v3). The editor picker uses the authenticated VisWiz v2 REST endpoint and supports editing the selected visualization in a new tab.

### Dataset manager

Datasets have a single management surface with:

- paginated/searchable dataset listing
- pre-aggregated row/node/relation/visualization counts (no nodes × relations × visualizations Cartesian count query)
- explicit dataset schema
- targeted row/node/relation editors
- graph validation
- graph preview
- JSON export/import
- dataset duplication
- WooCommerce snapshot replacement
- revision history and restore
- safe dataset deletion with visualization detachment

### Migration from 1.x

On the first 2.0.0 load, VisWiz creates the v2 tables and runs a one-time best-effort migration:

- existing `viswiz_datasets` rows are extended with v2 schema/source/revision metadata
- legacy `viswiz_data_points` are copied to v2 rows or nodes
- legacy `viswiz_relations` are copied to v2 edges using new immutable UUID references
- existing Visualization posts are mapped to `_viswiz_renderer`, `_viswiz_source_type`, `_viswiz_dataset_id` and `_viswiz_settings`
- recognized visualization-local manual/graph data without an existing dataset is migrated into a private dataset

The old 1.x data tables are deliberately **not dropped** by the migration. They remain available as a rollback/reference source until migration has been verified on the actual WordPress installation.

### Update system

The GitHub Releases updater is now an independent subsystem rather than the bootstrapper for the rest of the plugin.

- exact asset name required: `viswiz-{version}.zip`
- WordPress native per-plugin auto-update preference is authoritative
- `VISWIZ_AUTO_UPDATES` remains an optional hard override
- update metadata is cached for six hours and can be force-refreshed from VisWiz → Updates

### Tests and release validation

Pull requests and pushes to `main` now run:

- PHP syntax checks on all active/test PHP files
- JavaScript syntax checks
- PHPUnit domain + architecture regression tests
- block/composer JSON validation
- whitespace validation
- a real WordPress + MySQL integration smoke job
- WooCommerce installation in the smoke job
- activation / DB migration tests
- canonical graph storage tests
- node UUID/relation identity tests
- optimistic concurrency tests
- schema authorization tests
- dataset duplication/deletion tests
- WooCommerce live query smoke tests

The release workflow repeats source/unit validation before producing the installable ZIP and verifies the archive before publishing the GitHub Release.

Repository architecture
-----------------------

```
viswiz.php
src/
  Plugin.php
  Support.php
  Admin/
    Admin.php
  Database/
    Migrator.php
    DatasetRepository.php
  Domain/
    Registry.php
    GraphValidator.php
  Frontend/
    Frontend.php
  Rest/
    Api.php
  Update/
    GitHubUpdater.php
  WooCommerce/
    Compatibility.php
    SalesQuery.php
assets/
  viswiz.js
  viswiz.css
  viswiz-admin.js
  viswiz-admin.css
  viswiz-block.js
blocks/
  visualization/
    block.json
tests/
```

Operational note
----------------

2.0.0 is a major architectural release. The migration preserves 1.x tables, but a real wp-admin smoke test with the existing large graph datasets should be performed before the legacy tables are ever removed in a later release.
